                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#Terminology"} Terminology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#Motivation"} Motivation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Formulation"} Formulation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#structural_theorems"} Structural theorems
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_wightman_axioms"} Relation to Wightman axioms
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The Haag–Kastler axioms (
            %a{:href => "#HaagKastler64"}> Haag-Kastler 64
            ) (sometimes also called Araki–Haag–Kastler axioms) try to capture in a mathematically precise way the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (QFT), by axiomatizing how its algebras of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            should depend on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            regions, namely as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets of observables
          %p The main point of these axioms is to say that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                to every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+closed+subset"} causally closed subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} \subset X
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                there is associated a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})
            %li
              %p
                for every inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ↪
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_1 \hookrightarrow \mathcal{O}_2
                of such spacetime regions there is a corresponding inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_1) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_2)
            %li
              %p
                such that this respects
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                of inclusions (hence such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+closed+subsets"} causally closed subsets
                to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            %li
              %p
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"}> causal locality
                ) and such that whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_1, \mathcal{O}_2 \subset \mathcal{O} \subset X
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
                separated, then the elements of the corresponding algebras of observables (graded-)commute with each other:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                      %mn 0
                      %mphantom
                        %mi AAAA
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      [\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_1), \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O}_2)]
                      \= t
                      0
                      \\phantom{AAAA}
                      \\in \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})
                      \\,.
          %p Moreover one wants these assignments to behave well with spacetime symmetry.
          %p
            The formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            via these axioms has come to be known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+slice+axiom"} time slice axiom
          %p
            The Haag-Kastler axioms in their original form aim for the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of structural statements of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            or the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PCT+theorem"} PCT theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 4
            are missing.
          %p There are however variants:
          %ul
            %li
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
              one drops the requirement of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
              and instead considers just
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
              (in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              and connect to the traditional construction of pQFTs via the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
          %p
            The observation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            yields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            that satisfy the Haag-Kastler axioms, except that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            are replaced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"} Il’in-Slavnov 78
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09"} Brunetti-Dütsch-Fredenhagen 09
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#CausalLocality"} S-Matrix – Causal Locality and Quantum Observables
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+AQFT"} locally covariant perturbative AQFT
                one generalizes from
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
                to more general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
                in order to describe
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} QFT on curved spacetimes
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+AQFT"} homotopical AQFT
                one consider
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebras"} homotopical algebras
                and commutativity only up to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+law"} coherent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                in order to discuss
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                with non-trivial topological (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"}> instanton
                ) sectors.
          %h3#Terminology Terminology
          %p
            The approch to quantum field theory based on these axioms is often called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            \: either for
            %strong axiomatic quantum field theory
            (since it was among the first attempts to put the edifice of QFT on solid
            = succeed "atic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            %strong algebraic quantum field theory
            (since it amplifies the algebras of local
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            – see
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            – which, second, is also “algebraic” in some sense, even though algebras of observables to not appear directly.
          %p
            Another common term for these axioms is
            %strong local quantum field theory
            (see the title of the standard textbook (
            %a{:href => "#Haag"}> Haag
            )) since, as becomes clear
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Formulation"} below
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+quantum+field+theory"} extended
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            is all about the notion of locality of QFT.
          %p
            Therefore neither of the traditional terms for QFT as axiomatized by Haag-Kastler is truly descriptive in that it genuinely distinguishes from the other, the Atiyah-Segal axiomatization by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schreiber"} Schreiber
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#SatiSchreiber"} SatiSchreiber
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
                axiomatizes the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
                of QFT, which encodes the propagation of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                axiomatizes the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
                of QFT, which encodes the way that
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
          %h3#Motivation Motivation
          %p
            A central difference between the Haag-Kastler axioms and traditionally more widespread formulations of QFT (usually far from being formalized in any way) is the emphasis of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            of a QFT (Heisenberg picture) and the de-emphasis of the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"}> Hilbert
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"}> Schrödinger picture
            ). This emphasis receives motivation from the the fact that many technical problems of QFT simply disappear when one is not trying to form its spaces of states, while at the same time no real information about the theory is lost.
          %p Examples of technical problems that formulation in terms of spaces of states bring with them are the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                as opposed to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone-von+Neumann+theorem"} Stone-von Neumann theorem
                fails, making the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representation"} unitary representation
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
                on the spaces of states non-unique, hence requiring an explicit choice of representation. There is no generally good theory available for how to make this choice.
            %li
              %p
                More seriously,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag%27s+theorem"} Haag's theorem
                says that at a crucial step in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
                where one wants to pass from the representation “free fields” to that of “interacting fields”, the two representations are necessarily inequivalent, contrary to what is (silently or explicitly) assumed in much traditional QFT literature (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#EarmanFraser"} EarmanFraser
            %li
              %p
                In the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
                the formulation in terms of states brings with it
                %em infrared problems
                that are simply absent when formulating renormalization just in terms of observables (
                %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen"}> DuetschFredenhagen
                ).
          %h2#Formulation Formulation
          %p
            We formulate the ideas of the core axioms of Haag-Kastler, and their intended physical meaning. For more details see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong spacetime locality
              %p
                Since the
                %em fields
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                (such as the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
                %em local excitations
                (for instance the value of the electric/magnetic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                at any point) having effects only locally (the field excitations at two points a finite distance apart do not directly influence each other) the fields over any region of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                form a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsystem"} subsystem
                of the fields of any larger region and in particular of the total system.
              %p
                If “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"}> quantum mechanical system
                ” is formalized as “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"}> C-star algebra
                ” (of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsystem"} subsystem
                = succeed "-algebra\u201D." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebras"} C-star algebras
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
                whose co-restriction morphisms are
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                such is called an
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} isotonic net of algebras
                \.
              %p
                There are different approaches to define what kind of spacetime regions the algebras of observables are assigned to, hence different approaches as to what exactly the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                is on which the co-presheaf is defined. A common approach is to take all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+space"} bounded
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subspace"} open
                subsets of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            %li
              %p
                %strong causal locality
              %p
                If two regions of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
                separated, then there can be no influence between them whatsoever. Not only do the field excitations in one of the two regions not
                %em directly
                influence those in the other region (as per item 1), but they do not even influence
                %em indirectly
                \: no
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/waves"} waves
                of excitations (for instance
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+waves"} electromagnetic waves
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light"} light
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsystems"} subsystems
                constituted by these two regions accordording to the first point are even
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/independent+subsystems"} independent subsystems
                \. This is called
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
              %p
                The formalization of “two
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/independent+subsystems"} independent subsystems
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                is: two subalgebras that
                %em commute
                with each other inside the larger
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
                to their
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %p Therefore this translates into the axiom: quantum fields on a spacetime form an isotonic copresheaf of algebras such that the algebras assigned to any two spacelike separated regions commute with each other inside the algebra assigned to any larger region containing these two regions.
            %li
              %p
                %strong spacetime covariance
              %p The geometric symmetry operations map the algebra of a region onto the algebra of the transformed region.
              %p
                (this is not an extra axiom if one defines the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                of spacetime regions general enough…)
              %p
                In Minkowski spacetime the geometric symmetry group is usually taken to be the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            %li
              %p
                %strong positivity of energy
              %p
                An axiom is needed to ensure that only nonnegative energies occur – one possibility is the “spectrum condition”, which says that the spectrum (to be more precise: the support of the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+measure"} spectral measure
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#structural_theorems Structural theorems
          %p
            It is possible to prove both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PCT+theorem"} PCT theorem
            in the Haag-Kastler approach. The mathematically precise, model independent statements and their proofs are considered to be a major breakthrough of the theory.
          %h3#relation_to_wightman_axioms Relation to Wightman axioms
          %p
            Unlike the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"} Wightman axioms
            = succeed "\u201D:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+field"} field
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %strong Haag-Kastler axioms
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"}> local net of observables
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+AQFT"} locally covariant perturbative AQFT
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+theory"} modular theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article that introduced these axioms is
          %ul
            %li#Haag64
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"}> Rudolf Haag
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kastler"} Daniel Kastler
              = succeed "," do
                %em An algebraic approach to quantum field theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1704187"} doi:10.1063/1.1704187
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/9124"} spire:9124
          %p following
          %ul
            %li#Haag59
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"}> Rudolf Haag
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Discussion des “axiomes” et des propriétés asymptotiques d’une théorie des champs locales avec particules composées, Les Problèmes Mathématiques de la Théorie Quantique des Champs
          %p translated to English as:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"}> Rudolf Haag
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Discussion of the ‘axioms’ and the asymptotic properties of a local field theory with composite particles
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1140/epjh/e2010-10041-3"} doi:10.1140/epjh/e2010-10041-3
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Haag
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"}> Rudolf Haag
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Local+Quantum+Physics+--+Fields%2C+Particles%2C+Algebras"} Local Quantum Physics – Fields, Particles, Algebras
                Springer (1992) 2nd., rev. and enlarged ed. Springer (1996) (
                %a{:href => "https://www.springer.com/gp/book/9783540610496"}> ISBN 978-3-642-61458-3
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Huzihiro+Araki"}> Huzihiro Araki
                \:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathematical+Theory+of+Quantum+Fields"} Mathematical Theory of Quantum Fields
                Oxford University Press 1999
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0998.81501&format=complete"} ZMATH entry
          %p and seminar notes:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Garth+Warner"}> Garth Warner
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum Field Theory Seminar (School of Haag-Kastler et al.)
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://sites.math.washington.edu//~warner/QFT2Seminar_Warner.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Warner-HaagKastlerQFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Precursors include:
          %ul
            %li
              Bogolyubov, Logunov, Oksak, Todorov:
              %em General principles of quantum field theory.
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0732.46040&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
              )
          %p An online reference page is here:
          %ul
            %li
              Stephen J. Summers:
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ufl.edu/~sjs/aqft.html"} AQFT online
          %p
            See also the references at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
          %p
            One of the founding fathers of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            wrote:
          %ul
            %li#Dyson72
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freeman+Dyson"} Freeman Dyson
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Missed opportunities
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.uh.edu/~tomforde/Articles/Missed-Opportunities-Dyson.pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  These axioms, taken together with the axioms defining a C
                  %em
                    \-algebra are a distillation into abstract mathematical language of all the general truths that we have learned about the physics of microscopic systems during the last 50 years. They describe a mathematical structure of great elegance whose properties correspond in many respects to the facts of experimental physics. In some sense, the axioms represent the most serious attempt that has yet been made to define precisely what physicists mean by the words “observability, causality, locality, relativistic invariance,” which they are constantly using or abusing in their everyday speech.
                    = succeed "\u2026" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                    I therefore propose as an outstanding opportunity still open to the pure mathematicians, to create a mathematical structure preserving the main features of the Haag-Kastler axioms but possessing E-invariance instead of P-invariance.
              %p
                (on the latter see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
          %p
            The observation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            yields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            that satisfy the Haag-Kastler axioms, except that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            are replaced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#IlinSlavnov78
              %p
                V. A. Il’in and D. S. Slavnov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/135575"} spire
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01035870"} doi
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"} math-ph/9903028
            %li#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2038"} arXiv:0901.2038
          %p
            An introduction into
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tomita-Takesaki+modular+theory"} Tomita-Takesaki modular theory
          %ul
            %li
              Stephen J. Summers: “Tomita-Takesaki Modular Theory” (
              %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/math-ph/0511034"}> arXiv
              )
          %p and on its role in AQFT:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-J%C3%BCrgen+Borchers"}> Hans-Jürgen Borchers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On Revolutionizing of Quantum Field Theory with Tomita’s Modular Theory
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~esiprpr/esi773.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A discussion of how the Haag-Kastler axioms (those concerning locality) follow from an extended
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            with Lorentzian structure is in
          %ul
            %li#Schreiber
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                AQFT from
                = succeed "-functorial" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              , Comm. Math. Phys., Volume 291, Issue 2, pp.357-401 (
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/AQFTfromFQFT.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A discussion putting the state of the art of the
            = succeed "-axiomatization" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            = succeed "-axiomatization" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %ul
            %li#SatiSchreiber
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and String Theory
          %p
            A discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            in terms of the Haag-Kastler axioms is in
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              %em A local (perturbative)
              construction of observables in gauge theores: the example of qed_ , Commun. Math. Phys. 203 (1999), no.1, 71-105, (
              %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/ps/hep-th/9807078"}> arXiv:hep-th/9807078
              ).
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag%27s+theorem"} Haag's theorem
            and its meaning and implication is discussed thoroughly in
          %ul
            %li#EarmanFraser
              John Earman, Doreer Fraser,
              %em Haag’s theorem and its implications for the foundations of quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/2673/1/earmanfraserfinalrevd.pdf"}> pdf
              )
        .revisedby
